# G14-specific. These are listed as `aur` rather than `pac` on purpose: the
# asus-linux [g14] binary repo is frequently down or lagging behind the current
# kernel, and when it is, `pacman -S asusctl` fails with "target not found" and takes
# the whole transaction with it. The AUR packages build from the same sources and work
# whether or not the repo is configured. If you *do* have [g14] set up, yay finds the
# repo copies first and uses those, so this costs nothing.
aur asusctl
aur supergfxctl
aur rog-control-center

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not installed from here: GPU drivers
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `nvidia` vs `nvidia-dkms` vs `nvidia-open-dkms` depends on which kernel you're
# running, and picking wrong leaves you with no display — not the kind of decision to
# make from a package list that runs unattended. Install it deliberately, before
# Hyprland, and note that `nvidia-dkms` is the one that works here: the prebuilt
# `nvidia` package is compiled against the exact `linux` kernel version in the repos
# at build time, so it breaks the moment the two fall out of step. `nvidia-dkms`
# rebuilds the module against whatever kernel is actually installed.
#
#   sudo pacman -S nvidia-dkms nvidia-utils nvidia-settings linux-headers
#
# `linux-headers` is the part people miss — without it dkms has nothing to build
# against and silently produces no module.

